.SH DESCRIPTION
.B roster
reads an Inventory of Hosts from
.I roster.yml
and does one job per command.
Output is plain text, or JSON with
.BR \-\-json ,
and never contains a secret.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" "\fR, \fP\-\-config " FILE
Read the Inventory from
.IR FILE .
May appear before or after the command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print
.BI "roster " VERSION
and exit 0.
The rewrite carries on the Python roster's numbering: the first is 0.7.0.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B roster
With no command, print usage and exit 0, as
.B \-h
does.
.TP
.B pick
Open the picker: the
.B ls
lines in
.BR fzf (1),
with a preview of the Host under the cursor
.RB ( "roster ls \-\-long " \fIHOST\fP ).
Tab marks several Hosts.
Each Host picked runs
.BI "roster \-c " "FILE " "ssh " HOST,
with roster's own path and the Inventory's absolute path.
Inside tmux
.RB ( TMUX
set), Enter opens it in a new tmux window named after the Host and
.B ctrl\-v
in a split of the current one; marked Hosts open in one new window, a pane
each, tiled, with
.B synchronize\-panes
on, so what is typed goes to all of them.
Outside tmux, the pick replaces roster in place, as
.B ssh
does; marking several Hosts there exits 1.
Leaving fzf without a pick exits 0 and runs nothing.
Without fzf on
.BR PATH ,
roster says to install fzf and exits 1.
.I contrib/roster.tmux
binds tmux's
.B prefix+g
to the picker in a popup (edit the script to pick another key).
.TP
.B ls
Print one line per Host, in Inventory order, with the columns
name, group, address, access and title, padded and separated by two spaces.
There is no header line.
Access is
.B unreviewed
when the Host has
.BR "reviewed: false" ,
else
.B password
when it has
.BR "enroll: false" ,
else
.BR key .
.TP
.BI "ls " FILTER
Only the Hosts whose name, group,
.B type
or
.B product
equals one of the comma-separated words in
.I FILTER
(exactly, case and all):
.BR "roster ls hypervisors,printer" .
Nothing matched is exit 1, with a message on stderr.
.TP
.B ls \-\-long
Print each Host as
.I key: value
lines (name, group, address, access, title and description, blank ones
left out), with a blank line between Hosts.
For people and the picker's preview; scripts use
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B ls \-\-json
Print one JSON document:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"v": 1, "hosts": [{"name": "box\-a", "group": "dev",
  "ip": "10.0.0.5", "user": "admin", "port": 22,
  "access": "key", "title": "Box A", "description": "",
  "type": "", "product": "", "os": "posix",
  "key_type": "ed25519", "mesh": null,
  "web": null,
  "services": [{"name": "web", "port": 443,
    "url": "https://10.0.0.5", "scheme": "https",
    "user": "admin", "login": "basic", "verify": true}]}]}
.fi
.PP
Each field is the Host's resolved setting (see
.BR INVENTORY ).
.BR title ,
.BR description ,
.B type
and
.B product
are empty when unset;
.B mesh
is null when unset;
.B web
is null unless the Inventory gives a service a URL;
.B scheme
and
.B url
are null when roster can't tell.
A service's
.B auth
and the Host's are vault references and are never printed.
Within
.B \(dqv\(dq: 1
fields are only added, never renamed or removed.
No secret is ever included.
.RE
.TP
.B setup
Make this Box ready to reach the Hosts by name.
Each step is skipped when it's already done, so a rerun after editing
.I roster.yml
is quick and changes only what the edit changed; each change prints one line.
.RS
.IP 1. 4
The Box keys, one Box key per
.B key_type
the Enrolled Hosts use: each Host logs in with the Box key of its own
.B key_type
(see
.BR INVENTORY ).
When one is missing, it is made with
.BR "ssh\-keygen \-q \-t " ALG
.RB [ \-b
.IR BITS ]
.B \-N \(dq\(dq \-C roster\-managed:\c
.IR BOX ,
where
.I BOX
is
.BR box_name .
An existing key is never replaced.
.IP 2. 4
The ssh config,
.IR ~/.ssh/config.d/roster ,
mode 0600, written whole.
Its first line is
.BI "# Generated by roster from " PATH
with the Inventory's path; don't edit it by hand.
It has one block per Host, sorted by name:
.IP
.nf
# Box B [dev]
Host box\-b
    HostName 10.0.0.6
    User admin
    Port 22
    UserKnownHostsFile "~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts"
    StrictHostKeyChecking accept\-new
    ConnectTimeout 10
    ServerAliveInterval 30
    ControlMaster auto
    ControlPath "~/.ssh/cm\-%C"
    ControlPersist 10m
    IdentityFile "~/.ssh/roster_ed25519"
    IdentitiesOnly yes
.fi
.IP
A Password Host gets
.B PubkeyAuthentication no
in place of the
.B IdentityFile
line.
An
.B "os: windows"
Host gets
.B ControlMaster no
and no
.B ControlPath
or
.BR ControlPersist .
A % in an address is written %%.
.IP 3. 4
The line
.B "Include ~/.ssh/config.d/roster"
goes on the first line of
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
above any
.B Host
block, unless an
.B Include
of that file (in any form) is already there.
It is unquoted on purpose: bash\-completion's
.B ssh
host list doesn't follow a quoted
.B Include
path, so
.B ssh <Tab>
wouldn't offer the Hosts.
.IP 4. 4
.IR ROSTER.md ,
next to
.I roster.yml
(not in the working directory), mode 0644, written whole for Claude.
Its first line is
.BI "<!\-\- generated by roster from " FILE " \- regenerate with `roster sync` \-\->"
as in the Python roster, so the two can share it.
Then come how to connect (by access: key, password, unreviewed), the
rules (never read
.IR ~/.ssh ,
never fetch a password, ask before changing state), the groups, and
the Hosts as a table.
With more than 60 Hosts, a summary takes the table's place:
the count, and the counts by product and type.
The limit is built in; a
.B claude_inline_limit
in
.I roster.yml
is ignored.
A vault reference is never written: a service with a stored login is
marked
.BR (login) .
.IP 5. 4
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
next to
.IR roster.yml :
gets an
.B @ROSTER.md
line at its end, once; the rest is kept.
.IP 6. 4
.IR .claude/settings.json ,
next to
.IR roster.yml :
.B permissions.deny
gets
.B Read(~/.ssh/**)
and
.BR Edit(~/.ssh/**) ,
each added once.
Every other key is kept, and a file that denies both already is left
as it is.
A file that isn't valid JSON is left alone with a warning.
The user's own
.I ~/.claude
is never touched.
.IP 7. 4
The Hosts, one at a time, in Inventory order.
A Host that is unreviewed, has
.BR "os: windows" ,
or is a Password Host
.RB ( "enroll: false" )
is skipped, with one line on stderr giving the reason, and is never
contacted.
Mark a Windows Host
.B "os: windows"
(a
.B type:
is only a label): its shell has no sh, so the Mesh's scripts fail there
with
.IR "(a Windows Host? give it \`os: windows\`)" .
The probe that asks whether a Host takes the Box key runs
.BR exit ,
which every shell has.
For each other Host:
.RS
.IP a. 4
Its host key is recorded:
.B ssh\-keyscan \-T 5 \-p
.I PORT IP
is appended to
.I ~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts
(mode 0600), unless that file already has
.I IP
(or
.BI [ IP ]: PORT
off port 22).
No answer means the Host is
.BR down :
it is reported and left for the next run.
.IP b. 4
The probe:
.B ssh \-o BatchMode=yes \-o ControlMaster=no
.I HOST
.BR true .
When it succeeds the Host takes the Box key and there is nothing to do.
.IP c. 4
Otherwise roster resolves the Host's
.B auth
(see
.BR VAULT )
and logs in once with that password through askpass, with
.BR "\-o PubkeyAuthentication=no" .
A script on stdin
.RB ( "ssh " \fIHOST\fP " sh \-s" ;
nothing else is in argv) appends the line
.BI "TYPE KEY roster\-managed:" BOX
to
.IR ~/.ssh/authorized_keys ,
making
.I ~/.ssh
mode 0700 if it's missing.
The file is copied next to itself with its mode kept, the line is added
to the copy, and the copy is moved into place.
A line with the same key (whatever its tag, such as the legacy
.BR fleet\-managed: )
is never added twice.
An
.B auth
of
.BR agent ,
.B key
or
.B none
gives no password, so the key can't be installed that way.
.RE
.IP
A Host that is down, refuses the password, or fails the install is
reported on stderr and the run goes on with the next Host; at the end
roster says how many Hosts aren't done and exits 1.
Run
.B setup
again to finish them: Hosts already done cost one probe.
.IP 8. 4
The Mesh: Hosts that ssh to each other.
The Hosts of a group with
.B "mesh: true"
(set on the group, on a Host, or in
.BR defaults ;
.B "mesh: false"
on a Host opts it out) reach each other; groups don't reach each other.
Each
.B links
entry adds reach across groups: every Host of
.B from
reaches every Host of
.BR to ,
one way, or both ways with
.BR "both: true" ;
a pair already in a group's web isn't added twice.
Remove a Link and the next run removes its lines.
Only Enrolled, reviewed, POSIX Hosts without
.B seen_as
or
.B mesh_dir
(not supported yet) take part: any other Host with
.B "mesh: true"
or at an end of a Link is
.B left out of the Mesh
with the reason on stderr, in the plan and when applied.
A Link with
.B via
is skipped the same way, with the line
.BI "roster: link " FROM " \-> " TO " via " HOP ": skipped: via isn't supported yet" .
Nothing is kept on the Box: each Host that takes the Box key is visited,
one at a time, with
.B ssh \-o BatchMode=yes \-o ConnectTimeout=10
.I HOST
.BR "sh \-s" .
A read-only script reports the Host's Mesh key, its Mesh files and its
authorized_keys lines tagged
.BI roster\-mesh: SRC @ BOX\fR;
roster plans from that and the Inventory, and a second script makes the
plan so on a Host that needs changes.
On a source Host, in
.IR ~/.ssh/roster\-mesh/ ,
mode 0700:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the Mesh key,
.I id_ed25519
(or
.IR roster_mesh_<key_type> ),
made there with
.B ssh\-keygen \-C roster\-mesh:\c
.IB SRC @ BOX
when it's missing: the private key never leaves the Host;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR known_hosts :
the peers' host keys, copied from the Box's
.IR ~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts ;
.IP \(bu 2
.IR config :
one block per peer, with
.BR "StrictHostKeyChecking yes" ,
that known_hosts and the Mesh key.
Its first line is
.BI "# Written by roster mesh for " SRC " (owner " BOX ) ...
.RE
.IP
.I ~/.ssh/config
gets two lines at the top: the mark
.B # roster\-mesh: the Mesh's ssh config (roster mesh adds and removes these two lines)
and
.BI "Include " HOME /.ssh/roster\-mesh/config\fR.
.I ~/ROSTER.md
lists the peers for Claude there (first line
.BI "<!\-\- roster\-mesh: written by roster for " SRC " (owner " BOX );\fR...),
and when
.I ~/.claude
exists and the login user can write it,
.I ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md
gets
.BR @~/ROSTER.md .
A
.I ~/.claude
owned by someone else (root, after
.BR "sudo claude" )
gets no import; the rest of the Mesh is set up as usual.
Each peer's
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
gets one line per source:
.IP
.nf
from="IP",no\-agent\-forwarding,no\-X11\-forwarding TYPE KEY roster\-mesh:SRC@BOX
.fi
.IP
Every file is written to a copy beside it and moved into place.
These are v0.7.0's names and marks, so the Python roster sees the same
Mesh.
A Host that leaves the web (removed from
.IR roster.yml ,
or its
.B mesh: true
gone) loses its lines on the next run, and a Host that is no longer a
source loses its Mesh files, the Include and the import.
Ownership: only lines tagged
.BI @ BOX
are added or removed, only keys whose
.I .pub
carries that tag are deleted, and only files whose first line names this
Box as owner are rewritten or deleted.
A Mesh key roster didn't make, or another Box's Mesh config, is
.BR "left alone" :
that Host's own part is skipped and counts as not done.
A Host that is down is not done either; the lines of a source that
couldn't be read stay as they are on its peers until a run reaches it.
A rerun with nothing to change prints nothing.
.IP
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan and changes nothing, here or on a Host:
who reaches whom in the Mesh, one
.IB SOURCE " \-> " PEER
line each, then
.IB HOST ": would " CHANGE
for each Mesh change;
.BI "would write " PATH ,
.B would make the Box key
and, per Host,
.B would install the Box key on
.I HOST
or
.BR "would record the host key of " \fIHOST\fP " and install the Box key" .
It runs no ssh\-keyscan and contacts a Host only with the read-only probe,
and only when its host key is already recorded.
.PP
Every file is written atomically: to a temporary file in the same
directory, flushed to disk, then renamed into place, so an interrupted run
leaves the old file or the new one.
A file that exists keeps its mode.
.I ~/.ssh
and
.I ~/.ssh/config.d
are made mode 0700 if missing.
After
.BR setup ,
.BI "ssh " HOST
and anything that speaks ssh (rsync, an editor's remote mode)
reach a Host by its name.
.PP
.B setup \-\-undo
removes what
.B setup
put on the Hosts and the Box, for a user leaving or uninstalling roster,
and nothing else.
Each Enrolled, reviewed, POSIX Host is visited in turn with
.BR "ssh \-o BatchMode=yes" ,
using the Box key.
First its Mesh goes, as if no Host were in it: every authorized_keys line
tagged
.BI roster\-mesh: SRC @ BOX\fR,
the Mesh keys whose
.I .pub
carries that tag, and the Mesh config, known_hosts,
.IR ~/ROSTER.md ,
the Include and the import this Box wrote.
Last, since it cuts off roster's own way in, the Box key's line goes:
every authorized_keys line whose last field is exactly
.BI roster\-managed: BOX
or the legacy
.BI fleet\-managed: BOX\fR,
written to a copy beside the file and moved into place.
Other Boxes' lines and keys and lines added by hand are never touched.
A Host that refuses the Box key has nothing of this Box's roster can
reach, and counts as done.
A Host that is down, or whose Mesh cleanup fails, keeps its key line and
is not done: roster says how many Hosts aren't done and exits 1, and
.B setup \-\-undo
again finishes them.
.PP
Once every Host is done, the Box's files go:
.IR ~/.ssh/config.d/roster ,
the exact
.B "Include ~/.ssh/config.d/roster"
line (and an older roster's quoted
.BI "Include \(dq" HOME /.ssh/config.d/roster\(dq )
in
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
.IR ~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts ,
and, next to
.IR roster.yml ,
.IR ROSTER.md ,
the
.B @ROSTER.md
line in
.I CLAUDE.md
and the two deny rules in
.IR .claude/settings.json .
A file is deleted only when nothing else is left in it; a
.I ROSTER.md
or ssh config whose first line isn't roster's is left alone with a
warning.
The Box keys are kept, since they may still be needed elsewhere:
roster prints
.BI "kept the Box key " PATH
for each, and they are deleted by hand
.RB ( "rm ~/.ssh/roster_ed25519 ~/.ssh/roster_ed25519.pub" ).
.PP
Side by side with the Python roster v0.7.0, with the same
.BR box_name ,
each removes what the other wrote on the Hosts: v0.7.0's
.B "mesh cut \-\-all"
takes this roster's Mesh and its
.B revoke
the key lines, and
.B setup \-\-undo
takes v0.7.0's Mesh and key lines.
.PP
.B setup \-\-undo \-\-dry\-run
reads each Host and lists what would go, as
.IB HOST ": would remove " ...
and
.BI "would remove " PATH\fR,
and changes nothing.
.RE
.TP
.B sync
First, when Bitwarden is unlocked, import new Hosts from the vault into
.I roster.yml
(see
.B Hosts from the vault
under
.BR VAULT ).
Then rewrite the Box files from
.IR roster.yml :
steps 2 to 6 of
.B setup
(the ssh config, its
.BR Include ,
.IR ROSTER.md ,
.I CLAUDE.md
and the Claude settings), and nothing else.
It never contacts a Host and never makes a key: no ssh, ssh\-keygen or
ssh\-keyscan runs.
Hosts that are one login (the same address, port and user), say from a
hand edit, get a warning on stderr,
.IR "duplicate Hosts: A, B are all ADDRESS:PORT as USER; keep one" ,
and sync carries on.
When the Box key doesn't exist yet, the ssh config still names it in
.BR IdentityFile ,
where
.B setup
will make it.
A rerun with no edit changes nothing and prints nothing, so it suits cron:
.IP
.nf
*/15 * * * * roster sync
.fi
.IP
bash-completion's
.B ssh
completion reads
.B Host
names through the
.B Include
in
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
so
.BI "ssh " <Tab>
completes Hosts as long as
.B sync
keeps the file current.
.TP
.BI "add " NAME " \-\-ip " ADDR
Write a Host into
.I roster.yml
and run
.BR sync 's
Box steps, so
.BI "ssh " NAME
and
.BI "roster ssh " NAME
work at once.
.B \-\-group
names its group (default
.BR vault ,
made at the end of
.B groups:
if missing);
.BR \-\-user ,
.B \-\-port
and
.B \-\-auth
set those fields, and
.B \-\-enroll=false
marks a Password Host.
Nothing contacts the Host:
.B setup
installs the Box key.
The Host goes after the last one under its group's
.BR hosts: ,
as one line,
.IP
.nf
      box\-c: {ip: "10.0.0.9", port: 2222, user: "ops", auth: "bw:box\-c ssh", reviewed: true}
.fi
.IP
and every other line of the file stays as it was, comments included.
.B reviewed: true
is always written: adding a Host, or putting its login in the vault, is
its review, so
.B setup
takes it on even when
.B defaults
say
.BR "reviewed: false" ,
which keeps Hosts found other ways hands\-off.
roster refuses, writing nothing, when the name is taken, when
another Host is already the same login (the same address, port and user,
through
.B defaults
and the group:
.IR "box\-b is already 10.0.0.6:22 as admin" ;
the same address as another user, or on another port, is another login
and allowed), when
.BR groups: ,
the group or its
.B hosts:
is written in flow style
.RB ( "dev: {hosts: {...}}" ),
or when the result won't load.
The vault import skips such an item with the same reason.
A symlinked
.I roster.yml
is written through to its target.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-from\-vault " ITEM
Add the Host one vault item makes, by its name or id, under the rules of
.BR "Hosts from the vault" ,
even when its name doesn't follow the convention.
The Host is named
.BR \-\-name ,
else the item's HOST, else the item name made a valid name
.RB ( "wiki admin" " is " wiki\-admin ).
.B \-\-group
overrides the folder's group.
A name several items share is refused: give the id.
.TP
.BI "ssh " HOST " \fR[\fPARGS\fR...]\fP"
Replace roster with
.BI "ssh " "HOST ARGS"
for an Enrolled Host, using the block
.B setup
wrote.
.I ARGS
go to ssh unchanged, options and
.B \-\-
included:
.BR "roster ssh box\-b \-\- uptime" .
ssh's exit status is roster's.
.I HOST
is a Host's name exactly.
A name not in the Inventory exits 1 with
.BI "no Host named '" HOST "' in the Inventory" .
.IP
For a Password Host, roster resolves its
.B auth
reference (see
.BR VAULT )
and starts ssh as a child instead, giving it the password through askpass:
roster makes a directory only you can open (mode 0700) under
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
(else
.BR $TMPDIR ,
else
.IR /tmp )
with one Unix socket in it, and sets
.B SSH_ASKPASS
to the roster binary,
.B SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force
and
.B ROSTER_ASKPASS
to the socket.
When ssh asks, roster runs again as the askpass helper, reads the password
from the socket and prints it to ssh.
roster answers exactly one connection and then removes the directory
(also when something fails), so a wrong password isn't retried.
If ssh hasn't asked within 60 seconds, roster stops listening, removes the
directory and just waits for ssh.
The helper answers only a prompt that mentions a password or passphrase;
it refuses anything else, such as ssh's yes/no question about a new host
key.
The password is never in an argument, ssh's environment, a file or the
terminal.
A reference that can't be resolved exits 1 before ssh runs.
.BR agent ", " key " and " none
run ssh with no password, as for an Enrolled Host.
.IP
No
.I HOST
is a usage error.
.TP
.BI "run " "TARGET " "\-\- " CMD
Run
.I CMD
on every Host
.I TARGET
names, in parallel:
.BR "roster run dev,esx\-02 \-\- uptime" .
.I TARGET
is Host and group names, comma-separated, in any mix; each Host runs once.
A name that is neither exits 1 with
.BI "no Host or group named '" NAME "' in the Inventory"
before anything runs.
Unreviewed Hosts are skipped, each with
.BI "roster: skipped " HOST ": unreviewed"
on stderr; nothing left to run exits 1.
.I CMD
and its arguments go to the Host's shell as
.B ssh
joins them, so quote what the local shell shouldn't expand.
.IP
Each Host gets
.BI "ssh \-o BatchMode=yes \-o ConnectTimeout=10 " "HOST " "\-\- " CMD
(a Password Host without
.BR BatchMode ,
which would stop ssh asking askpass), with stdin from
.IR /dev/null .
At most 32 Hosts run at once (a constant in the source).
The connect timeout means a down or unreachable Host fails after 10 seconds
instead of holding up the others; a command that hangs on a Host still
does.
Each line a Host prints goes out as it arrives, prefixed
.BI "" HOST ": "
\(em stdout lines to stdout, stderr lines to stderr; lines from different
Hosts don't mix within a line, but their order across Hosts is arrival order.
.IP
Password Hosts get their passwords through askpass, as for
.BR ssh .
Every Password Host's
.B auth
is resolved before any Host starts, so prompts come one at a time and a
reference that won't resolve exits 1 before any ssh runs.
No ssh, a key Host's included, inherits an
.B env:
variable a password came from.
.IP
roster exits 1 if any Host failed (a non-zero exit, a signal, or ssh that
couldn't start), after
.BI "roster: failed on " "HOST" ", ..."
on stderr; else 0.
.TP
.B run \-\-json
Print nothing until every Host is done, then one JSON document, Hosts in
.I TARGET
order:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"v": 1, "hosts": [
  {"host": "box\-a", "exit": 0, "stdout": "...", "stderr": ""}
]}
.fi
.PP
.B exit
is the Host's ssh exit status (255 when ssh itself failed, such as a
connect timeout), or null when ssh was killed by a signal or couldn't start.
.B stdout
and
.B stderr
are the whole output, unprefixed; bytes that aren't UTF\-8 are replaced.
Skipped Hosts are not listed. The exit status is as without
.BR \-\-json .
.RE
.TP
.BI "check " [TARGET]
Explain why a Host can't be reached, or show the fleet's health at a glance.
.I TARGET
is as for
.BR run ;
with none, every Host in the Inventory is checked.
Each Host goes through these stages in order, stopping at the first that
fails:
.RS
.TP
.B dns
.B ip
resolves through the system resolver.
.TP
.B port
A TCP connection to the first address and
.B port
opens within 5 seconds.
.TP
.B login
.BI "ssh \-o BatchMode=yes \-o ConnectTimeout=10 " HOST " exit"
succeeds
.RB ( exit
works in sh, cmd and PowerShell, so an
.B "os: windows"
Host checks too), with stdin from
.IR /dev/null ;
on failure, ssh's own error is shown.
No ssh inherits an
.B env:
variable a password came from.
.TP
.B vault
For a Password Host, instead of
.BR login :
its
.B auth
reference resolves (see
.BR VAULT ).
The password is never shown.
References are resolved one at a time before the network checks, so a
.B cmd:
that asks for an unlock asks once.
.B prompt
is not asked for: the Host passes with
.BR "(auth prompt: not checked here)" .
.RE
.IP
Hosts are checked in parallel, at most 32 at once, and printed in
.I TARGET
order, one line each, then a summary:
.RS
.PP
.nf
box\-a: ok
box\-b: login: admin@10.0.0.6: Permission denied (publickey).
esx\-02: ok
3 Hosts: 2 ok, 1 failed
.fi
.RE
.IP
Unreviewed Hosts are checked too. roster exits 1 if any Host failed, else 0.
.TP
.B check \-\-json
One JSON document instead, Hosts in the same order:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"v": 1, "hosts": [
  {"host": "box\-a", "ok": true, "failed": null, "error": null, "note": null},
  {"host": "box\-b", "ok": false, "failed": "port",
   "error": "10.0.0.6:22: Connection refused (os error 111)", "note": null}
]}
.fi
.PP
.B failed
is the stage that failed
.RB ( dns ", " port ", " login " or " vault ),
.B error
says why, and
.B note
says what wasn't checked. The exit status is as without
.BR \-\-json .
.RE
.TP
.B vault unlock
Unlock Bitwarden once for the day.
roster runs
.BR "bw unlock \-\-raw \-\-passwordenv ROSTER_BW_MASTER" ,
with the master password from
.B BW_PASSWORD
or asked on
.IR /dev/tty ,
and set only in bw's environment, never in an argument.
An empty master password fails at once with
.IR "no master password given" ,
before bw runs.
Run it from a terminal: without one there is no
.I /dev/tty
to ask on.
The session key goes into the kernel keyring with
.BR keyctl (1):
a
.B user
key
.B roster:bw\-session
in the user keyring
.BR @u ,
which expires after 12 hours.
It is never written to a file.
Without
.B keyctl
nothing can be kept: roster says to install keyutils, exits 1, and each
.B bw:
reference asks for the master password.
.TP
.B vault lock
Purge the key from the keyring and run
.BR "bw lock" .
.IP
Both delete roster 0.7's
.I ~/.config/roster/bw\-session
file if it is there, and say so; roster never reads it.
.TP
.BI "web " "HOST " "\fR[\fPSERVICE\fR]\fP " "METHOD PATH"
Call a Host's web API with
.BR curl (1)
and the service's stored Login, without the caller ever seeing the
credential.
.I SERVICE
is a name under the Host's
.BR services ;
without it, the one
.B ls
shows as
.BR web :
the
.B web
service (or the legacy
.B web:
field), else the first service with a URL.
.I METHOD
is upper case, such as GET or POST.
.I PATH
must start with
.B /
and stay on the service's origin (its scheme, address and port; any path
in the service's URL is dropped): no
.BR // ,
.BR :// ,
.BR .. ,
spaces or backslashes.
.IP
curl's arguments hold nothing secret: roster runs
.B curl \-q \-sS \-\-proto =http,https \-\-max\-redirs 0 \-w ... \-K \-
.BI "\-X " "METHOD URL" ,
so redirects are not followed and
.I ~/.curlrc
is not read.
The Login goes to curl as a config on its stdin
.RB ( "\-K \-" ),
never in argv:
.B user = """USER:PASSWORD"""
for
.B login: basic
(the service's
.BR user ),
or
.B header = """Authorization: Bearer TOKEN"""
for
.BR "login: bearer" .
.B login: none
sends no Login, and nor does an
.B auth
of
.BR agent ,
.B key
or
.BR none .
The credential is resolved as in
.BR VAULT ,
and no
.B env:
variable of the Host's reaches curl's environment.
.IP
The response body goes to stdout and curl's errors to stderr.
roster exits 0 when the HTTP status is below 400, else prints
.BI "roster: " "HOST SERVICE" ": HTTP " STATUS
(or
.B no HTTP response
when curl failed) and exits 1.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-d " BODY"
Send
.I BODY
as the request body, in the stdin config, so an
.B @
in it is not special.
.BI \-d " @FILE"
reads the body from
.I FILE
.RB ( "\-\-data\-binary @FILE" );
.B \-d @\-
is refused, since stdin carries the Login.
.TP
.BI \-H " HEADER"
Add a request header; repeatable.
.TP
.B \-k
Don't verify the TLS certificate, as for a service with
.BR "verify: false" .
.RE
.TP
.B help plugins
Print the plugin contract below, with an example.
.B help
alone prints usage.
.TP
.B version
The same as
.BR \-\-version .
.TP
.I NAME
.RI [ ARGS ...]
Any other name runs a plugin; see
.BR PLUGINS .
.SH PLUGINS
Anyone can extend roster in any language, git style:
.B roster
.I NAME ARGS
runs the first executable
.BI roster\- NAME
on
.B PATH
with
.I ARGS
unchanged.
Options after
.I NAME
belong to the plugin;
.B \-c
.I FILE
before it picks the Inventory.
roster replaces itself with the plugin, so the plugin's exit status is
roster's.
.PP
Built-in commands always win: a
.B roster\-ls
on
.B PATH
that would shadow
.B ls
is ignored with a warning.
A name that is neither built in nor on
.B PATH
is a usage error (exit 2).
.PP
The plugin's environment adds:
.TP
.B ROSTER_CONFIG
the resolved Inventory path, absolute; unset when no Inventory is found,
since a plugin may not need one.
.TP
.B ROSTER_BIN
roster's own path, to call back into it.
.PP
A plugin reads Hosts with
.BR "\(dq$ROSTER_BIN\(dq ls \-\-json" ,
whose
.B \(dqv\(dq
field versions the format (see
.BR "ls \-\-json" ).
Plugins never get secret values: not in the environment, not in
.BR \-\-json .
An example, saved as
.B roster\-names
on
.B PATH
and made executable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/bin/sh
"$ROSTER_BIN" ls | cut \-d' ' \-f1
.fi
.RE
.PP
More examples, unsupported, are in
.I contrib/
in the source.
.SH INVENTORY
The Inventory is the first of:
.IP 1. 4
the
.B \-c
.IR FILE ;
.IP 2. 4
.BR $ROSTER_CONFIG ;
.IP 3. 4
.I ./roster.yml
in the current directory;
.IP 4. 4
.IR ~/.config/roster/roster.yml .
.PP
It holds
.BR groups ,
each with
.B hosts
by name.
A Host is a mapping of settings, or just its address as a string:
.PP
.RS
.nf
box_name: lab\-box
defaults: {user: admin}
groups:
  dev:
    title: Dev boxes
    ssh: {user: deploy, auth: "bw:dev deploy"}
    hosts:
      box\-a: {ip: 10.0.0.5, title: Box A, services: {web: 443}}
      box\-b: 10.0.0.6
links:
  \- {from: box\-a, to: box\-b, both: true}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A setting is looked up on the Host, then its group, then
.BR defaults ,
then roster's built-in value.
At each of those layers, an
.B ssh:
block of
.BR user ,
.B port
and
.B auth
beats the plain keys of the same name.
A group's own
.BR title ,
.B description
and
.B hosts
describe the group and are not inherited.
.PP
Top-level keys:
.TP
.B groups
Required: group name to its settings and
.BR hosts .
A Host name is letters, digits, . _ and \-, and a Host is in one group.
.TP
.B box_name
This Box's name in key tags (default: the short hostname); characters other
than letters, digits, _ and \- become \-.
An empty name (with no hostname to fall back on) is refused.
.TP
.B key_type
This Box's key type:
.BR ed25519 " (default), " ecdsa " (P-384), " ecdsa\-256 ", " ecdsa\-521 ,
.B rsa
(3072 bits) or
.BI rsa\- BITS
(3072 to 16384).
.TP
.B key_path
This Box's key file (default:
.IR ~/.ssh/roster_<key_type> ),
the one the Hosts of the top-level
.B key_type
use.
A Host of another type uses
.IR roster_<type> " beside it"
.RI ( ~/.ssh/roster_ecdsa
for a FIPS-mode Host with
.BR "key_type: ecdsa" ,
which refuses Ed25519).
A
.B key_path
that names another type's key is refused: remove it or point it at another file.
.TP
.B links
A list of
.BR "{from, to, both, via}" :
each end a Host or a group,
.B both
true for two-way, and
.B via
a Host that isn't an end (not supported yet: the Mesh skips such a Link).
A name that is both a Host and a group can't be an end.
.PP
Host settings:
.TP
.B ip
Required. No space, quote or control character; a % only as an IPv6 zone
.RI ( fe80::1%eth0 ).
.TP
.BR user ", " port ", " auth
The ssh login.
.B user
is required somewhere in the chain and holds no space, quote or %;
.B port
defaults to 22;
.B auth
(default
.BR prompt )
is a vault reference, only ever read when used (see
.BR VAULT ).
.TP
.BR enroll ", " reviewed
Both default to true.
.B "enroll: false"
is a Password Host;
.B "reviewed: false"
an unreviewed one, found by a scan.
.TP
.BR os ", " key_type ", " mesh
.B os
is
.B posix
(default) or
.BR windows ;
.B key_type
as above (default: the top-level one);
.B mesh
true, false or unset: whether the Host is in its group's Mesh (see
.BR setup ).
.TP
.BR title ", " description ", " type ", " product
Free text on one line.
The legacy
.B notes
stands in for an unset
.BR description .
.TP
.B services
Name to a port
.RB ( "db: 5432" ),
a URL
.RB ( "ui: https://10.0.0.5:8443" ),
or
.BR "{port, url, scheme, user, auth, verify, login}" .
Entries merge field by field down the layers.
Ports 443 and 8443 mean https, and 80, 8000, 8008, 8080 and 8888 http.
.B login
is
.BR basic " (default), " bearer " or " none ;
an entry roster can't use is left out.
The legacy
.BI "web: " URL
is a service named web.
.PP
Other keys, such as
.BR resources ,
.BR sudo ,
.B shell_hooks
or
.BR workers ,
are loaded and ignored.
.PP
A true-or-false setting
.RB ( enroll ,
.BR reviewed ,
.BR mesh ,
.BR verify ,
.BR both )
also takes
.BR yes / no ,
.BR on / off
and
.BR y / n ,
in any case, as v0.7.0 reads them; anything else is an error
(a service with a bad
.B verify
is left out).
.PP
An invalid Inventory stops every command with exit 3 and one message
.RI "in the form " "FILE:LINE: KEY: why" ,
for example
.IR "roster.yml:7: groups.dev.hosts.box\-a.ip: can't hold a space" .
.SH VAULT
A Host's or service's
.B auth
is a reference to its password, resolved only when a command needs it.
The password is held in memory that is wiped after use, and roster never
prints it.
Hosts that share a reference resolve it once per run
.RB ( prompt
asks once per Host), and Bitwarden is unlocked at most once.
.TP
.BI cmd: COMMAND
The first line
.BI "sh \-c " COMMAND
prints, for any password manager's CLI:
.BR "cmd:pass show net/sw\-01" .
The command's stdin and stderr are the terminal's, so it can ask to be
unlocked.
A non-zero exit or an empty line is an error.
.TP
.BI env: VAR
The environment variable
.IR VAR ,
for CI.
roster keeps
.I VAR
(and every other Host's, and
.BR BW_PASSWORD " and " BW_SESSION )
out of the environment of each ssh and curl it starts.
.TP
.B prompt
Ask on
.I /dev/tty
with echo off.
The default.
.TP
.BI bw: ITEM\fR[\fB#\fIFIELD\fR]
Bitwarden's item
.IR ITEM ,
through
.BR "bw get item " \fIITEM\fP " \-\-nointeraction" ,
with the session key from the keyring (see
.BR "vault unlock" )
as
.B BW_SESSION
in bw's environment only.
Without
.I FIELD
it is the login password;
.B #username
is the login user name,
.B #notes
the notes, and any other
.I FIELD
a custom field, matched exactly, then ignoring case.
A missing or expired key means Bitwarden is locked: roster says to run
.B roster vault unlock
and unlocks for this run only, with the master password from
.B BW_PASSWORD
or
.IR /dev/tty ,
storing nothing.
roster keeps
.B BW_PASSWORD
out of ssh's environment.
.IP
Security: the session key is never on disk, in a backup or in a synced
repo, and it expires on its own (12 h, or
.BR "vault lock" ).
Its permissions are
.BR 0x3f0b0000 :
the owner's processes may view, read and search it, so
.B roster sync
from cron (in a keyring session of its own) can import from the vault;
none of them may change, replace or relink it or extend its life;
group and other users get nothing.
So any process running as the same user can read it with
.BR "keyctl print" ,
just as it could read a file; no other user can.
.TP
.BR agent ", " key ", " none
No password.
.PP
Any other reference, such as
.BI op: ITEM
or
.BI kp: ITEM ,
is an error that says to use
.B cmd:
with that tool's CLI.
.SS Hosts from the vault
Add a login item in Bitwarden named
.IB "HOST " ssh
or
.IB "HOST " ssh+ X
.RB ( ssh
in any case, HOST a valid Host name), and the next
.B roster sync
adds the Host to
.IR roster.yml ,
printing
.BI "imported " HOST " from the vault (group " G ) .
No hand edit is needed.
The import runs only with a session key in the keyring (see
.BR "vault unlock" ):
it runs
.BR "bw sync" ,
.B bw list items
and
.BR "bw list folders" ,
each with
.BR \-\-nointeraction ,
and never prompts, so it works from cron.
Without a session key, or without
.B bw
or
.BR keyctl ,
it prints one line,
.BR "vault import skipped" ,
on stderr, and the rest of sync runs.
.PP
An item is imported when it is a login item, follows the convention, and
no Host has the name HOST and no
.B auth:
names the item yet.
Its first URI gives the address and port:
.BI ssh:// USER @ HOST : PORT\fR,\fP
.IB HOST : PORT\fR,\fP
a bare HOST, or an
.BR http (s)
URL's host with port 22.
Its user name is the Host's
.BR user ,
and
.B auth
is
.BI bw: "ITEM NAME"\fR,\fP
or
.BI bw: ID
when other items share the name.
The group is the item's folder, lowercased with other characters made
.B \-
.RB ( "Lab Servers" " is " lab\-servers ),
or
.B vault
without a folder.
An item with no address in its first URI is skipped with one line on stderr.
The Host is written as
.B add
writes one.
.PP
Only item names, ids, folders, URIs and user names are read: an item's
password is never parsed, printed or written.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing matched the
.IR FILTER ;
.B ssh
was given a Host it can't reach, or a Password Host whose
.B auth
won't resolve;
.B run
failed on a Host, was given an unknown
.IR TARGET ,
or had no reviewed Host to run on;
.B check
found a Host failing or was given an unknown
.IR TARGET ;
.B web
got an HTTP status of 400 or more, or no response, or was given an unknown
Host or service; a
.B setup
step failed, or a Host wasn't done (down, refused the password, or
the install failed), or with
.B \-\-undo
a Host wasn't done (down, or its Mesh cleanup failed);
.B vault unlock
or
.B lock
failed, or found no keyctl; or the picker can't run fzf or tmux.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown command or option, or a missing value.
An unknown command is one that is neither built in nor a
.BI roster\- NAME
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B 3
The Inventory won't load: no file was found, it can't be read, or it is
invalid.
The message names the file.
.TP
.B 126
A plugin, or ssh, was found but can't be run, curl can't be started, or
the askpass socket
can't be made.
.PP
A plugin's exit status, and ssh's for
.BR "roster ssh" ,
is passed through unchanged.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BW_PASSWORD
The Bitwarden master password, for
.B vault unlock
and a locked
.B bw:
reference; else it is asked on
.IR /dev/tty .
.TP
.B BW_SESSION
Set by roster in bw's environment only; roster ignores one you set.
.TP
.B ROSTER_CONFIG
The Inventory path, when
.B \-c
is not given.
Set by roster for a plugin to the resolved path.
.TP
.B ROSTER_BIN
Set by roster for a plugin to roster's own path.
.TP
.B ROSTER_ASKPASS
Set by roster for ssh's askpass helper to the socket path.
roster started with it set is that helper, nothing else.
.TP
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ", " TMPDIR
Where the askpass directory goes, in that order, else
.IR /tmp .
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for
.BI roster\- NAME
plugins, and for fzf and tmux.
.TP
.B TMUX
Set inside tmux; the picker then opens Hosts in tmux windows and panes.
.TP
.B HOME
Used to find
.IR ~/.config/roster/roster.yml ,
.I ~/.ssh
and the Box key.
.SH COMPLETION
.I contrib/roster.bash
completes
.B roster
in bash: the commands, each command's flags, the words after
.B vault
and
.BR help ,
a file after
.BR \-c ,
and Host and group names for
.BR ssh ,
.BR run ,
.BR check ,
.BR web ,
.BR ls .
The names come from
.B roster ls
at each Tab (honouring
.BR \-c ),
so a Host added to
.I roster.yml
completes at once, with nothing to regenerate.
When
.I roster.yml
won't load, no names complete and nothing is printed.
.B make install
in the source tree installs it as
.IR $PREFIX/share/bash\-completion/completions/roster ,
next to the binary and this page
.RI ( $PREFIX/share/man/man1/roster.1 ),
with
.I PREFIX
.I ~/.local
by default;
.B make uninstall
removes all three.
Without
.BR make ,
copy it there by hand, or add
.B source /path/to/roster.bash
to
.IR ~/.bashrc .
.PP
For plain
.BR ssh ,
bash\-completion reads
.B Host
names through the
.B Include
in
.IR ~/.ssh/config ;
run
.B roster sync
(from cron, say) to keep that file current.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/roster_<key_type>
The Box keys, one per key type in use, and their
.IR .pub ;
the top-level type's is at
.B key_path
when set.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config.d/roster
The ssh config written by
.B setup
and
.BR sync .
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config
Gets the
.B Include
line, once.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts
roster's own known hosts, named in each Host block;
.B setup
appends what ssh\-keyscan finds.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/roster\-mesh/
On a Host in the Mesh: its Mesh key, known_hosts and config, with
.I ~/ROSTER.md
beside them (see
.BR setup ).
.TP
.IR ROSTER.md ", " CLAUDE.md ", " .claude/settings.json
Next to
.IR roster.yml ;
what Claude reads, written by
.BR setup .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR fzf (1),
.BR tmux (1)
